==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{etydate|1450}}, in {{w|Middle English}}; from {{inh|en|enm|intoxicaten}}, from {{af|enm|intoxicat|-en|alt1=intoxicat(e)|t1=(''of a weapon or drug'') smeared, anointed or filled with poison; (''of a human being, animal'') poisoned, intoxicated|pos1=also used as the past participle of ''intoxicaten''|id2=infinitival|pos2=verb-forming suffix|nocap=1|nocat=1}}, borrowed from {{m|la-med|intoxicātus}}, {{glossary|perfect}} {{glossary|passive}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|la-med|intoxicō}} (see {{af|en|-ate|id1=verb|pos1=verb-forming suffix}}), from {{der|en|la-lat|toxicō||to smear, anoint with poison}}, from {{m|la|toxicus||toxic, poisonous}} + {{m|la|-ō|id=denominative|pos=verb-forming suffix}}, from {{der|en|grc|τοξικόν}}. {{surf|+con|en|in|toxic|ate|id1=into|nocat=1}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈtɒksɪkeɪt/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈtɑksɪkeɪt/|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-intoxicate.wav|a=Southern England}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To [[stupefy]] by [[dope|doping]] with [[chemical]] [[substance]]s such as alcohol.
#: {{syn|en|drunken|get drunk|inebriate}}
# To excite to enthusiasm or madness.
#: {{syn|en|awaken|exhilarate|Thesaurus:thrill}}

